%% ---- X-F1 (sections/app_P_persys.tex); printed text kept in the paper: see CUT_APX.md
The limit Table~\ref{tab:persys} quotes for a system is that of its best
window, so a system whose coverage is wide is not penalised for the
shallowest window in it; the window-by-window distribution is the per-window
result of \S\ref{sec:pninetysel}. Each row is one system, a gravitationally
bound pair appearing under its primary's name. A window inside the attribution mask
constrains nothing (\S\ref{sec:summary}) and cannot be a best window: a
double dagger marks a row keeping less than half its channels, and
\NSysALostName{}, whose \UmNWinALostWin{} windows are all inside it, is
tabulated with dashes. Beside each limit the table
gives that window's centre frequency $\nu$ and native channel width
$\Delta\nu$, the system's merged coverage $\Delta\nu_{\rm cov}$, the
percentage \emph{dec.} by which atmospheric decorrelation leaves the limit
optimistic, and whether two blocks of different observing sessions cover a
common frequency more than a day (d) or more than a year (yr) apart, over the
primary census together with the \ZcNRecEB{} blocks the recurrence
test of \S\ref{sec:etacrv} reads, of which \ZcNRecEBOut{} lie outside the
census -- \ZcNRecEBOutExt{} in the archival extension, \ZcNRecEBOutHold{} in
the reserved hold-out and \ZcNRecEBOutEpoch{} in the epoch-extension
campaign. The frequency at which a system's best limit falls is set by what the
archive holds toward it and not by any property of the star, and for
\ZcNoRepSys{} of the \NSysClassA{} systems no sensitivity would have been
enough.

%% ---- X-F2 (sections/app_P_persys.tex); printed text kept in the paper: see CUT_APX.md
\emph{Because a system's limit is its best window, a minimum over several
estimates could favour windows whose own completeness factor falls below the
class average}: drawing each window's factor from the \SensNInj{} measured
ones instead, over \ScRsNTrial{} trials, shifts the per-system median by
\ScRsMedShiftPct{} per cent of the adopted $\EirpNinetyMultA$. A dagger marks
the \SmcNMeasSys{} of the \NSysALim{} systems whose own best window was
one of the \SensNInj{} injected into; the other \SmcNEstSys{} limits are
transferred from the class average and carry the dispersion observed between
injected windows, $\times\SmcDispLo$ to $\times\SmcDispHi$, and for the
\NgNExtrapSys{} \NgExtrapSysNoun{} whose best window lies outside the injected
range in band, channel width, array configuration or integration time that
dispersion is itself transferred. A dagger marks where the class factor was
calibrated by injection, not a different convention: every limit, daggered or
not, carries the one class factor.

